WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.259
Is scaling slowing?

2
00:00:01.539 --> 00:00:02.840
The named positions.

3
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.613
“Is scaling hitting a wall?

4
00:00:04.893 --> 00:00:11.251
” has been asked every year since at least 2022, and the honest answer depends on which curve you mean.

5
00:00:11.531 --> 00:00:15.435
Here are the positions you will meet, each with its date and its evidence.

6
00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:19.811
Pretraining returns are diminishing; the field is moving to research.

7
00:00:20.091 --> 00:00:38.117
Ilya Sutskever, in the November 2025 interview above, argued that the era of scaling a known recipe (roughly 2020 to 2025) is ending, partly because data is finite, and that new ideas are needed, especially for generalization.

8
00:00:38.397 --> 00:00:53.736
Supporting evidence: Epoch AI’s estimates (Villalobos and colleagues, revised 2024) that frontier training could use up the stock of high-quality public human text between 2026 and 2032.

9
00:00:54.016 --> 00:01:05.728
Evidence against: labs have kept improving models through synthetic data, RL environments, and test-time compute, so “pretraining is harder” has not meant “models stopped improving.

10
00:01:06.008 --> 00:01:06.306
”.

11
00:01:06.586 --> 00:01:08.868
Scaling never delivered understanding.

12
00:01:09.148 --> 00:01:18.748
Gary Marcus has argued since his March 2022 essay “Deep Learning Is Hitting a Wall” that scaling alone would not produce reliable reasoning.

13
00:01:19.028 --> 00:01:22.740
Lesson 5 covers his and other critics’ positions in depth.

14
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:27.522
Evidence for: persistent reliability problems, documented in week 3.

15
00:01:27.802 --> 00:01:37.935
Evidence against: many specific capabilities he and others predicted would not arrive (strong coding, competition math) did arrive with scale plus RL.

16
00:01:38.215 --> 00:01:41.095
Progress is continuing and may be accelerating.

17
00:01:41.375 --> 00:01:55.988
Dario Amodei said in a February 2026 interview with Dwarkesh Patel that he believes “we are near the end of the exponential” (the episode’s title), meaning close to very capable systems rather than at a plateau.

18
00:01:56.268 --> 00:02:12.951
In his September 2026 essay “We Must Pace the Frontier” he went further, arguing that progress had sped up since roughly mid-2026 because AI systems increasingly help build their successors, and calling for the industry to slow down.

19
00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:15.727
Evidence for: METR’s Time Horizon 1.

20
00:02:16.007 --> 00:02:31.772
1 update (January 2026) estimated a doubling time for task length of about 131 days since 2023, faster than the roughly seven months reported in 2025 (lesson 2 examines this).

21
00:02:32.052 --> 00:02:45.044
Evidence against: those measurements cover mostly software tasks and METR itself says its suite becomes unreliable above about 16 hours; and the claimant runs a lab whose business depends on the answer.

22
00:02:45.324 --> 00:02:46.412
The careful middle.

23
00:02:46.692 --> 00:03:05.210
The International AI Safety Report 2026 (February 2026, over 100 contributing experts) summarizes the state of evidence rather than taking a side: capabilities kept improving, increasingly through inference-time techniques and post-training,

24
00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:08.924
while forecasts of future progress remain highly uncertain.

25
00:03:09.204 --> 00:03:12.703
That framing is the one this elective recommends you start from.
